        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I personally store session files in designated directory inside `~/.config/nvim`. That directory is git-ignored because: a) it is a major privacy issue to have possible file paths (even relative) out in the public; b) it is not that hard to reproduce session manually if you need to.\r\n\r\nNot sure I remember a dedicated `SessionWrite(Post)` event. Quick googling didn't result into anything also. What I would do here depends on the exact use case:\r\n- If you want to patch automatically, you could use a `VimLeavePre` event (which ['mini.sessions'](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/8c278019a68c80724ac05e55139b3a73d718dd75/lua/mini/sessions.lua#L81) itself is using). Make sure to create your autocommand *after* `require('mini.sessions').setup()`.\r\n- If you call `MiniSessions.write()` manually, than write a wrapper which calls `MiniSessions.write()` and then makes your desired changes.\r\n- A **very dirty** hack is to emulate hook by reassigning `MiniSessions.write()`. It *should* work for both previous cases. So something like this *should* work (not tested):\r\n```lua\r\nrequire('mini.sessions').setup()\r\n\r\n_G.original_write = MiniSessions.write\r\n\r\nMiniSessions.write = function(...)\r\n  _G.original_write(...)\r\n  -- Your code goes here\r\nend\r\n```",
            "created_at": "2022-04-29T15:06:14Z",
